<DocsLayout
  title="Modelling & estimation"
  description="A precise guide to the modelling paths, transformer integrations, uncertainty evidence, and parameter-estimation limits in super-charge-ml."
  current="modeling"
>
  <p class="eyebrow"><span></span> Models, uncertainty, and limits</p>
  <h1>Choose the model<br /><em>after the evidence.</em></h1>
  <p class="lead">super-charge-ml can search and evaluate several model families, including an optional FT-Transformer path, and can pretrain a bounded tabular JEPA representation. It also exposes a separate, auditable parameter-estimation contract. Those are useful building blocks—not a claim that every dataset, transformer, or scientific question is production-ready.</p>

  <div class="callout"><p><strong>Read this page before installing extras.</strong> The supported beta is still the narrow tabular library workflow. Deep-learning and text-embedding integrations are available as explicit optional paths with separate evidence. Tabular JEPA is experimental; multimodal JEPA is not implemented. Parameter estimation is a separate scientific evidence track that can be attached to an <code>MLProject</code> run, but it never changes model selection.</p></div>

  <h2>The decision path</h2>
  <figure class="diagram">
    <figcaption class="diagram-caption"><span>MODEL SELECTION</span><strong>A larger model never repairs an untrusted dataset.</strong></figcaption>
    <div class="flow-row">
      <div class="flow-node"><span>01</span><b>Understand</b><small>identity · labels · leakage</small></div>
      <i class="flow-arrow">→</i>
      <div class="flow-node"><span>02</span><b>Screen</b><small>baselines · constraints</small></div>
      <i class="flow-arrow">→</i>
      <div class="flow-node active"><span>03</span><b>Compare</b><small>CV · calibration · cost</small></div>
      <i class="flow-arrow">→</i>
      <div class="flow-node"><span>04</span><b>Explain</b><small>limits · sensitivity · review</small></div>
    </div>
    <div class="evidence-rail"><span>release evidence</span><i></i><span>package · infer · monitor</span></div>
  </figure>
  <p>The planner owns the comparison: it screens model families against task, data shape, resource limits, and declared policy. A candidate is not a release because it has the highest point score. The run must retain the split identity, trial parameters, metrics, uncertainty, calibration diagnostics, explanation evidence, package verification, and known limitations.</p>

  <h2 id="onboarding">A developer’s first pass</h2>
  <p>Start with the question and the raw dataset boundary, not with a model name. The shortest useful path is: run the <a href={`${base}/docs/quickstart/`}>tabular quickstart</a>, inspect the <a href={`${base}/docs/data/`}>curation and dataset identity</a>, choose a supported capability in the <a href={`${base}/docs/capabilities/`}>capability matrix</a>, then use the <a href={`${base}/docs/api/`}>Python API reference</a> to make the declaration explicit. Only after that should you compare optional model families.</p>
  <div class="doc-cards">
    <a class="doc-card" href={`${base}/docs/quickstart/`}><small>01 / RUN</small><h3>Make a first tabular run</h3><p>See the smallest end-to-end project and the artifacts it leaves behind.</p></a>
    <a class="doc-card" href={`${base}/docs/data/`}><small>02 / UNDERSTAND</small><h3>Audit the raw input</h3><p>Confirm source identity, labels, missingness, leakage, and split eligibility before fitting.</p></a>
    <a class="doc-card" href={`${base}/docs/api/`}><small>03 / DECLARE</small><h3>Use the public API</h3><p>Declare modality, target, policy, and evidence boundaries in Python.</p></a>
    <a class="doc-card" href={`${base}/docs/release-evidence/`}><small>04 / PROMOTE</small><h3>Read the release boundary</h3><p>Know which local checks still do not justify a production claim.</p></a>
  </div>

  <h2 id="support-matrix">What modelling exists today</h2>
  <p>The matrix below is a claim boundary, not a catalogue of packages that happen to import. “Supported” means the path is the explicitly gated beta workflow. “Experimental” means there is a usable, tested seam with modality-specific limitations. “Extension-only” means the caller owns the missing lifecycle and evidence.</p>
  <div class="model-table" role="region" aria-label="Modeling support matrix" tabindex="0">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Path</th><th>How to use it</th><th>Current claim</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Tabular beta</th><td>Use <code>MLProject</code> with a declared target and tabular input. The planner can compare the built-in linear, tree, and other candidate families under the supported workflow.</td><td><span class="status status-supported">Supported*</span></td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">FT-Transformer</th><td>Install <code>super-charge-ml[deeplearning]</code> and explicitly opt in with <code>ModelSpec(experimental_models=["torch_ft_transformer"])</code>. The adapter then enters the normal project plan, cross-validation, package, and prediction path after dependency, row-limit, and capability checks.</td><td><span class="status status-experimental">Experimental</span></td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Other PyTorch families</th><td>The same optional extra exposes MLP, residual, TabM-style ensemble, and RealMLP-style families. They are explicit candidates, not silent dependencies.</td><td><span class="status status-experimental">Experimental</span></td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Text embeddings</th><td>Install <code>super-charge-ml[embeddings]</code> for the sentence-transformers integration. The text transformer runs in the isolated worker; if unavailable, the documented fallback is TF-IDF.</td><td><span class="status status-experimental">Experimental</span></td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">TabPFN</th><td>The explicit adapter remains extension-only until the external package, model-weight license, token custody, and independent evidence are supplied.</td><td><span class="status status-extension">Extension-only</span></td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Tabular JEPA</th><td>Install <code>super-charge-ml[deeplearning]</code> and explicitly opt in with <code>ModelSpec(experimental_models=["torch_jepa"])</code>. The project adapter composes the self-supervised <code>JepaEstimator</code> with a supervised head, so the same representation is evaluated, packaged, loaded, and used by <code>RunResult.predict</code>.</td><td><span class="status status-experimental">Experimental</span></td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Parameter estimation</th><td><code>estimate_parameters</code> supports declared linear, logistic, Bayesian Gaussian, binary and continuous-treatment IPW/AIPW paths, and grouped random-intercept or one-random-slope estimators, with explicit assumptions, uncertainty, sensitivity, and independent reference evidence. Clustered uncertainty names its sampling units and diagnostics. Reports are digest-bound and can be persisted with <code>EstimationResult.write</code>.</td><td><span class="status status-experimental">Bounded API</span></td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Longitudinal causal estimation</th><td><code>LongitudinalIPWEstimator</code> accepts validated subject/time rows and estimates period-specific binary-treatment Hájek IPW effects with overlap, effective sample size, subject-aligned covariance, and digest-bound reports.</td><td><span class="status status-experimental">Bounded API</span></td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Sequential causal estimation</th><td><code>SequentialIPWEstimator</code> estimates a static always-treated versus always-control contrast over two or more periods using stabilized treatment-history weights and declared pre-treatment time-varying covariates.</td><td><span class="status status-experimental">Bounded API</span></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="note">* “Supported” is intentionally narrow: it describes the explicitly gated tabular library path, not every dataset, model, accelerator, or deployment environment.</p>

  <h2 id="transformers">Try an optional transformer path</h2>
  <p>The extra is a deliberate boundary. It keeps the base install small and makes the dependency, worker process, resource cost, and failure mode visible in the run. Start with a small budget and compare the transformer against a simple baseline on the same split.</p>
  <pre><code>{`from scml import MLProject
from scml.contracts.tasks import ModelSpec

# The opt-in is part of the hashed project configuration and the run plan.
project = MLProject(
    data,
    target="label",
    models=ModelSpec(
        experimental_models=["torch_ft_transformer", "torch_jepa"]
    ),
)
plan = project.plan()
result = project.run(plan)
predictions = result.predict(new_rows)
result.save("bundle", signing_key="load-this-from-your-secret-store")`}</code></pre>
  <p>Install the optional runtime before running this example: <code>pip install "super-charge-ml[deeplearning]"</code>. Before treating the result as useful, inspect the run evidence for the resolved adapter, dependency versions, seed, train/validation/test identity, resource observations, failed trials, and explanation limitations. An explicit opt-in makes the path runnable; it does not make it production-supported. There is no general transformer registry, distributed training guarantee, hosted weight registry, or production text-understanding claim behind this optional extra.</p>

  <h2 id="jepa">Pretrain a tabular JEPA representation</h2>
  <p>JEPA is a transformer-adjacent self-supervised representation workflow, not a target-label model. The project integration accepts a tabular feature plan, masks the resulting finite numeric matrix, predicts an EMA target representation, and trains a small supervised head on the embeddings. The downstream benchmark, split, and model card remain your responsibility.</p>
  <pre><code>{`from scml.models.deeplearning import JepaEstimator

encoder = JepaEstimator(
    embedding_size=64,
    mask_probability=0.3,
    epochs=50,
    random_state=42,
)
embeddings = encoder.fit_transform(numeric_features)

# Evaluate the representation with a declared downstream split.
downstream_model.fit(embeddings[train], labels[train])
score = downstream_model.score(embeddings[test], labels[test])`}</code></pre>
  <p>This is experimental and opt-in only. It does not decode raw documents, images, audio, video, graphs, or geospatial objects; it does not provide a universal pretrained checkpoint; and it is never automatically included in supervised model search. A project package contains the fitted representation and head together, so normal bundle loading and prediction are reproducible within the declared environment.</p>

  <h2>JEPA is implemented narrowly, not implied broadly</h2>
  <p>The repository now has an explicit tabular JEPA path. It uses numeric feature masking, a context encoder, an EMA target encoder, an embedding-space prediction objective, and variance regularization to make collapse observable. It runs in the isolated PyTorch worker and returns a representation through <code>transform</code>.</p>
  <div class="split-cards">
    <div class="mini-card"><span class="mini-label">Today</span><h3>What is honest</h3><p>Tabular numeric JEPA is an experimental representation trainer and an explicit <code>MLProject</code> candidate named <code>torch_jepa</code>. It has a defined objective, serialized worker state, deterministic seed, capability gate, package path, and prediction test.</p><p class="note">The representation still needs downstream transfer benchmarks and independent domain review.</p></div>
    <div class="mini-card"><span class="mini-label">Still separate</span><h3>What is not included</h3><p>There is no document, image, audio, video, graph, or geospatial JEPA encoder; no universal pretrained checkpoint; and no claim that the embedding is semantically meaningful without a domain benchmark.</p><p class="note">A new modality needs its own encoder, masking policy, transfer test, and explanation boundary.</p></div>
  </div>

  <h2 id="boundaries">What the current modelling claim does not include</h2>
  <p>A typed input or a passing unit test is not the same as a production model family. Use this boundary when deciding whether to build on the library or write an extension.</p>
  <div class="model-table" role="region" aria-label="Unsupported modelling boundary" tabindex="0">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Area</th><th>What exists</th><th>Do not infer</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Transformers</th><td>Optional tabular FT-Transformer and text-embedding adapters with explicit dependency and resource gates.</td><td>General-purpose multimodal transformers, foundation-model weights, distributed training, or production serving are not covered.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">JEPA</th><td>A tabular numeric representation trainer with masking, an EMA target encoder, checkpoint state, and <code>transform</code> output.</td><td>There is no image, document, audio, video, graph, or geospatial JEPA; no universal checkpoint; and no semantic transfer guarantee.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Non-tabular data</th><td>Experimental curation, typed manifests, bounded baselines, and extension contracts for document, image, audio, video, graph, and geospatial families.</td><td><code>MLProject</code> is not a production non-tabular modelling path. OCR, layout understanding, image representation, audio/video understanding, GNNs, and geospatial foundation models remain outside the supported claim.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">External runtime</th><td>Local evidence can record package, inference, and recovery checks within the tested boundary.</td><td>Cloud, container, kernel, broker exactly-once, hosted CI, public deployment, and independent review evidence are not created by a local run.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="note">When a requirement appears in the third column, use the <a href={`${base}/docs/extensions/`}>extension contract</a> and keep the resulting capability marked extension-only until its own evidence exists.</p>

  <h2 id="selection-vs-estimation">Parameter estimation is not hyperparameter tuning</h2>
  <p>This distinction matters. The current project has useful pieces, but they answer different questions:</p>
  <div class="split-cards">
    <div class="mini-card"><span class="mini-label">Implemented</span><h3>Choosing a model</h3><p>Hyperparameter search explores declared spaces. Bayesian model comparison evaluates repeated score differences. Calibration and conformal methods quantify predictive behaviour. Cross-validation can report intervals around metrics.</p></div>
    <div class="mini-card"><span class="mini-label">Implemented, bounded</span><h3>Estimating a parameter</h3><p><code>EstimandSpec</code>, <code>EstimationPolicy</code>, and <code>EstimationResult</code> record the parameter definition, likelihood, assumptions, covariance or posterior uncertainty, intervals, sensitivity evidence, and reference calculation. Causal and hierarchical paths require explicit assumptions and report their unproven boundaries.</p></div>
  </div>
  <figure class="diagram">
    <figcaption class="diagram-caption"><span>THREE DIFFERENT QUESTIONS</span><strong>Do not read one as another.</strong></figcaption>
    <div class="planes">
      <div class="plane"><span>Predictive uncertainty</span><strong>What might this model predict for a new case?</strong><p>Conformal intervals, prediction sets, calibration diagnostics.</p></div>
      <div class="plane plane-execution"><span>Model selection uncertainty</span><strong>How stable is the comparison between candidates?</strong><p>Repeated folds, seed intervals, Bayesian score comparisons.</p></div>
      <div class="plane plane-evidence"><span>Statistical parameter uncertainty</span><strong>What is the uncertainty around a defined population quantity?</strong><p>Estimand, identification, estimator, covariance, standard errors, sensitivity.</p></div>
    </div>
  </figure>

  <p>Suppose a search chooses logistic regression because it predicts held-out conversions well. The selected model’s coefficient is a property of that fitted procedure; it is not automatically the population log-odds effect you meant to estimate. A parameter-estimation run must name the population quantity, the data-generating likelihood, the assumptions that identify it, and the uncertainty calculation before it reports a number.</p>

  <h2 id="parameter-contract">The parameter-estimation contract</h2>
  <p>Read an <code>EstimationResult</code> as a compact scientific record. Each part answers a different review question:</p>
  <div class="model-table" role="region" aria-label="Parameter estimation contract" tabindex="0">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Evidence</th><th>What it says</th><th>What a reviewer still asks</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row"><code>estimand</code></th><td>Name, outcome, population, observation window, likelihood, features, and declared assumptions.</td><td>Is this the quantity the decision-maker actually needs, and does the sample represent that population?</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Identifiability</th><td>Assumptions such as conditional exchangeability, positivity, consistency, or random-intercept exogeneity are recorded explicitly.</td><td>Which assumptions are design commitments, which can be checked against data, and which remain untestable domain claims?</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row"><code>parameters</code></th><td>Point estimates, standard errors, intervals, and p-values in the declared coefficient order.</td><td>Does the sign and scale have a domain interpretation, and are the intervals being read as confidence or credible intervals?</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row"><code>covariance</code></th><td>A square covariance matrix for the reported parameters. Its diagonal is the squared standard error; its off-diagonal terms preserve dependence between estimates.</td><td>Is IID, HC3, clustered, posterior, influence-function, or model-based uncertainty appropriate for this design?</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row"><code>sensitivity</code></th><td>Evidence for missingness handling, bootstrap or prior changes where supported, overlap, shift, clipping, and other declared perturbations.</td><td>Would a plausible change in sampling, missingness, prior strength, or distribution shift change the conclusion?</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row"><code>reference</code></th><td>An independent calculation or solver check against the primary implementation, with tolerance and comparison details.</td><td>Did a second route agree within tolerance, and is that route genuinely independent rather than a duplicate helper?</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Lineage</th><td>Rows used and dropped, raw-input fingerprint, serialized digest, and—when attached to a project—an artifact in the run evidence graph.</td><td>Can another evaluator retrieve the same raw snapshot, policy, software lock, and report without reconstructing intent from logs?</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="uncertainty">How to read uncertainty without overclaiming</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Start with the estimand.</strong> A 95% confidence interval describes repeated-sampling behaviour under the declared estimator and assumptions. A Bayesian credible interval describes posterior probability under the declared likelihood and prior. Neither one repairs an unidentified question.</li>
    <li><strong>Read covariance as a matrix, not decoration.</strong> Standard errors describe individual coordinates; covariance describes how estimates move together. Robust or clustered covariance changes the uncertainty calculation, not the underlying population definition.</li>
    <li><strong>Separate sensitivity from uncertainty.</strong> A narrow interval under one policy can coexist with a large change under a different missingness rule, prior, overlap threshold, sample, or target population.</li>
    <li><strong>Give the reference calculation a veto.</strong> If the independent calculation disagrees beyond the declared tolerance, treat the result as a failed diagnostic—not as a rounding difference to hide.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="api-onboarding">Run a declared estimate</h2>
  <p>The estimation API is intentionally explicit. You supply the design matrix, outcome, scientific declaration, and covariance policy; the result tells you what was estimated, how many rows were used, how uncertainty was computed, and whether the independent reference calculation agreed. This example is self-contained apart from NumPy and the installed package:</p>
  <pre><code>{`import numpy as np

from scml import EstimandSpec, EstimationPolicy, EstimationResult
from scml import estimate_parameters

features = np.array([
    [0.0, 1.0], [1.0, 1.0], [0.0, 2.0], [1.0, 2.0],
    [0.0, 3.0], [1.0, 3.0], [0.0, 4.0], [1.0, 4.0],
], dtype=float)
outcome = np.array([1.2, 2.1, 1.7, 2.6, 2.2, 3.0, 2.8, 3.5])

estimand = EstimandSpec(
    name="conversion_score_association",
    outcome="conversion_score",
    population="eligible visitors observed in 2026",
    likelihood="gaussian",
    feature_names=("treatment", "prior_visits"),
    assumptions=("rows are conditionally independent",),
)
policy = EstimationPolicy(
    estimator="linear",
    covariance="hc3",
    confidence_level=0.95,
    seed=7,
)
report = estimate_parameters(features, outcome, estimand, policy)
report.write("artifacts/parameter-estimation.json")
verified = EstimationResult.read("artifacts/parameter-estimation.json")
print(verified.parameters)
print(verified.covariance)
print(verified.sensitivity)
print(verified.reference)`}</code></pre>
  <p>That report is not automatically a causal conclusion. It is a reproducible estimate under the assumptions you declared. For a treatment effect, use <code>bayesian_ate</code>, <code>ipw_ate</code>, or <code>aipw_ate</code> with explicit identification assumptions. For grouped observations, use <code>hierarchical_linear</code> with explicit random-intercept assumptions, or add one declared <code>random_slope_position</code> with its matching <code>random_slope</code> assumption. These methods report what they did and what they did not prove; an independent domain review is still required.</p>

  <h2 id="project-integration">Attach the estimate to an MLProject run</h2>
  <p>Use the project API when the estimate must travel with the same tabular snapshot and run evidence as model selection. The estimation policy does not choose the predictive model, and the predictive model does not silently define the estimand.</p>
  <pre><code>{`from scml import MLProject

project = MLProject(
    data=frame,
    target="conversion_score",
    task="regression",
    modality="tabular",
    estimand=estimand,
    estimation_policy=policy,
    output_dir="runs/conversion-score",
)
result = project.run()
assert result.estimation is not None
print(result.run_dir / "parameter_estimation.json")`}</code></pre>
  <p>The run binds the digest-verified <code>parameter_estimation.json</code> artifact into evaluation and lineage. Missing, malformed, or tampered reports fail closed. Resume and replay reconstruct the signed declaration and rerun the scientific track; they do not infer an undeclared question from logs.</p>

  <h2 id="longitudinal">Estimate period-specific effects safely</h2>
  <p>Use <code>LongitudinalIPWEstimator</code> when each subject contributes ordered rows at several periods and you want a separate effect per period. The contract requires unique subject-time identities, baseline covariates, treatment support, overlap, and explicit assumptions about consistency, positivity, exchangeability, and time-varying confounding.</p>
  <pre><code>{`from scml import LongitudinalIPWEstimator
from scml import LongitudinalEstimationPolicy
from scml import LongitudinalEstimand

longitudinal_estimand = LongitudinalEstimand(
    name="period_specific_treatment_effect",
    subject_identity="subject_id",
    time_column="period",
    treatment_column="treated",
    outcome="outcome",
    covariates=("baseline_score",),
    population="eligible subjects observed in each study period",
    assumptions=(
        "consistency",
        "positivity",
        "conditional_exchangeability",
        "no_unmeasured_time_varying_confounding",
        "no_time_varying_covariates",
    ),
)
report = LongitudinalIPWEstimator(
    longitudinal_estimand,
    LongitudinalEstimationPolicy(seed=17),
).fit(long_frame)
report.write("artifacts/longitudinal-ate.json")`}</code></pre>
  <p>This is deliberately not a sequential g-formula or marginal structural model. It refits a period-specific propensity model and reports the boundary in <code>limitations</code>. If treatment history, time-varying covariates, or sequential interventions matter, the current contract is insufficient and the capability must remain extension-only.</p>

  <h2 id="sequential">Estimate a sustained treatment-history contrast</h2>
  <p>Use <code>SequentialIPWEstimator</code> when the scientific question is a static regime contrast—every period treated versus every period untreated—and the data contains one complete, ordered row per subject and period. At each period, the denominator model conditions on observed treatment history, baseline covariates, and declared pre-treatment time-varying covariates. A numerator model conditions on treatment history to stabilize the cumulative weights.</p>
  <pre><code>{`from scml import SequentialEstimand
from scml import SequentialEstimationPolicy
from scml import SequentialIPWEstimator

sequential_estimand = SequentialEstimand(
    name="sustained_treatment_effect",
    subject_identity="subject_id",
    time_column="period",
    treatment_column="treated",
    outcome="outcome",
    baseline_covariates=("baseline_score",),
    time_varying_covariates=("current_risk",),
    population="subjects with complete follow-up",
    assumptions=(
        "consistency",
        "positivity",
        "sequential_exchangeability",
        "no_unmeasured_time_varying_confounding",
        "time_varying_covariates_are_pre_treatment",
        "complete_follow_up",
    ),
)
report = SequentialIPWEstimator(
    sequential_estimand,
    SequentialEstimationPolicy(maximum_weight=20.0, seed=17),
).fit(long_frame)
report.write("artifacts/sequential-ate.json")
`}</code></pre>
  <p>The report retains per-period propensity support, cumulative-weight truncation, effective sample size, regime estimates, uncertainty, subject/row accounting, a raw-input fingerprint, and an independent normalized-weight reference. This is not a dynamic treatment-regime estimator, g-computation, or marginal structural model. Intermittent follow-up, continuous treatment within this sequential contract, and propagated propensity-model uncertainty remain outside the contract.</p>

  <h2 id="continuous-treatment">Estimate a continuous-treatment dose response</h2>
  <p>Use <code>continuous_ipw_ate</code> when treatment is numeric and the scientific quantity is a one-unit marginal outcome change. The estimator fits an explicit Gaussian generalized propensity score—treatment conditional on every declared non-treatment feature—then uses stabilized marginal-to-conditional density weights for a weighted linear dose-response regression.</p>
  <pre><code>{`from scml import EstimandSpec
from scml import EstimationPolicy
from scml import estimate_parameters

estimand = EstimandSpec(
    name="dose_response_slope",
    outcome="outcome",
    population="eligible observations in the study window",
    likelihood="gaussian",
    feature_names=("dose", "age", "risk_score"),
    assumptions=("the declared covariates precede treatment assignment",),
)
policy = EstimationPolicy(
    estimator="continuous_ipw_ate",
    covariance="clustered",
    cluster_name="site_id",
    treatment_position=0,
    continuous_weight_cap=20.0,
    continuous_treatment_assumptions=(
        "consistency",
        "conditional_exchangeability",
        "continuous_treatment_positivity",
        "linear_dose_response",
        "normal_conditional_treatment_density",
    ),
)
report = estimate_parameters(
    features,
    outcome,
    estimand,
    policy,
    clusters=site_id,
)
report.write("artifacts/continuous-treatment.json")
`}</code></pre>
  <p>Inspect <code>report.diagnostics["overlap"]</code> and <code>report.diagnostics["weights"]</code> before interpreting the slope. They include observed treatment support, conditional-density scale, raw and capped weight ranges, capped fraction, weight quantiles, and effective sample size. The result says <em>diagnosed, not proven</em> for positivity: a cap controls numerical influence but cannot repair unsupported treatment regions. The current contract does not estimate a nonlinear dose-response, continuous-treatment AIPW, or propagated generalized-propensity uncertainty.</p>

  <h2 id="estimator-matrix">Choose the scientific estimator deliberately</h2>
  <div class="model-table" role="region" aria-label="Parameter estimation methods" tabindex="0">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Estimator</th><th>Use it for</th><th>Evidence and limits</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row"><code>linear</code> / <code>logistic</code></th><td>Declared Gaussian or Bernoulli associations.</td><td>IID, HC3, or clustered covariance; intervals, missingness and shift diagnostics, optional bootstrap sensitivity, and an independent reference calculation.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row"><code>bayesian_linear</code></th><td>A Gaussian design with a declared conjugate normal-inverse-gamma prior.</td><td>Posterior covariance, credible intervals, prior-strength sensitivity, and an independent positive-definite solve. Robust, clustered, Bayesian logistic, and Bayesian bootstrap variants fail closed.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row"><code>bayesian_ate</code></th><td>A binary-treatment ATE from a conjugate Gaussian outcome model with covariate adjustment.</td><td>Requires linearity, consistency, conditional exchangeability, and positivity assumptions; reports posterior covariance, Student-t credible intervals, prior sensitivity, treatment-arm counts, and an independent dense solve. Time-varying effects, nuisance uncertainty, clustered covariance, and fully Bayesian multilevel models remain unsupported.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row"><code>ipw_ate</code></th><td>A binary-treatment average treatment effect under a declared propensity model.</td><td>Hájek IPW, clipping and overlap diagnostics, effective sample sizes, influence-function variance, and an independent propensity reference. This does not prove exchangeability, positivity, or absence of unmeasured confounding.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row"><code>aipw_ate</code></th><td>A binary-treatment average treatment effect with propensity and outcome nuisance models.</td><td>Augmented IPW score, clipping and overlap diagnostics, influence-function variance, fitted-nuisance limitations, and an independent propensity-plus-outcome reference. Cross-fitting and nuisance-model uncertainty propagation are not implemented; this remains a bounded scientific estimator.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row"><code>continuous_ipw_ate</code></th><td>A one-unit marginal dose-response slope for a continuous treatment under a Gaussian generalized propensity density.</td><td>Stabilized density-ratio weights, raw/capped weight diagnostics, overlap status, effective sample size, IID or declared-cluster uncertainty, an explicit capability declaration, and an independent dense reference. Nonlinear dose-response, continuous-treatment AIPW, and nuisance-model uncertainty propagation remain unsupported.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row"><code>LongitudinalIPWEstimator</code></th><td>Separate period-specific binary-treatment effects from long-format subject/time observations.</td><td>Requires unique ordered subject-time rows, baseline covariates, overlap, effective sample-size bounds, and explicit longitudinal assumptions. It is not a sequential g-formula or marginal structural model; treatment-history effects and time-varying covariates remain unsupported.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row"><code>SequentialIPWEstimator</code></th><td>A static always-treated versus always-control contrast over a complete treatment history.</td><td>Uses stabilized history weights and explicit pre-treatment time-varying covariates. Dynamic regimes, intermittent follow-up, continuous treatment, g-computation, marginal structural models, and propensity-model uncertainty propagation remain unsupported.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row"><code>hierarchical_linear</code></th><td>Grouped Gaussian outcomes with a random-intercept assumption, optionally plus one declared random slope.</td><td>Choose bounded <code>moment_gls</code> or explicit <code>reml</code> variance-component estimation. Inspect covariance, cluster evidence, optimizer/reference diagnostics, and stated uncertainty limits. Multiple slopes, nested/crossed effects, fully Bayesian multilevel inference, and variance-component uncertainty propagation remain out of scope.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="note">The estimator is part of the claim. Do not label a model-selection coefficient as a scientific parameter estimate. For <code>aipw_ate</code>, opt into <code>cross_fitting_folds</code> when held-out nuisance scoring is required; the report still identifies what uncertainty has not been propagated.</p>

  <h2>What you can inspect now</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Search decisions:</strong> the plan records the candidate family, parameter space, budget, pruning decisions, and failed or deferred trials.</li>
    <li><strong>Predictive evidence:</strong> classification and regression runs can retain calibration diagnostics and conformal prediction intervals when the policy and data volume allow them.</li>
    <li><strong>Comparison evidence:</strong> repeated evaluation can report score intervals and a decision instead of pretending a single split is certainty.</li>
    <li><strong>Explanation evidence:</strong> linear contributions, permutation-style reports, and bounded perturbation checks are recorded with method parameters and known representation limits.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>These artifacts help you decide whether a model is useful. They do not turn a predictive score into a causal estimate, and they do not supply a treatment effect, coefficient standard error, Bayesian posterior, or identified population parameter.</p>

  <h2>Parameter-estimation acceptance bar</h2>
  <p>To promote this capability into a release workflow, the API must answer these questions without reconstructing intent from logs:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>What exact estimand is being estimated, for which population and observation window?</li>
    <li>What assumptions identify it, and which assumptions are testable versus declared?</li>
    <li>Which estimator and optimization procedure ran, with the resolved parameters and convergence diagnostics?</li>
    <li>How was uncertainty estimated: robust covariance, bootstrap, profile likelihood, or posterior—and why?</li>
    <li>Can another evaluator reproduce the estimate from raw snapshot, split, seed, software lock, and artifact digest?</li>
    <li>What sensitivity, subgroup, missing-data, and leakage checks would change the interpretation?</li>
  </ul>
  <div class="callout"><p><strong>Current answer:</strong> predictive modelling and a bounded estimation evidence track are real and can be persisted from <code>MLProject</code>. The estimator contracts cover transparent Gaussian/Bernoulli, conjugate Bayesian, Bayesian ATE, IPW/AIPW ATE, and grouped random-intercept or one-random-slope paths; stronger causal identification, advanced Bayesian models, time-varying effects, and modality-specific estimators remain separate work.</p></div>

